# AI Usage Tracker — Cursor Project Rules
# EC2 deployment (Docker + PM2)

## Project Overview
A B2B SaaS web app that tracks AI provider costs (OpenAI, Anthropic, GCP, AWS).
Shows spend dashboards, sends budget alert emails, runs daily cost-fetch cron jobs.
Target users: CTOs, engineers, finance leads managing multi-provider AI spend.

## Tech Stack
- Framework: Next.js 14 (App Router) with standalone output mode
- Styling: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
- Database + Auth: Supabase (@supabase/supabase-js + @supabase/ssr)
- Email: Resend (@resend/react + react-email)
- Cron: EC2 cron job (hits /api/cron/fetch-usage daily)
- Error tracking: Sentry
- Deployment: EC2 via Docker + PM2

## Critical Next.js Config for Docker
next.config.js must have:
  output: 'standalone'
This is required for the Dockerfile to work. Without it, the build fails.

## Folder Structure
src/
  app/
    (auth)/
      login/page.tsx
      signup/page.tsx
    (dashboard)/
      layout.tsx          # sidebar + auth guard
      dashboard/page.tsx  # spend overview
      providers/page.tsx  # manage connected providers
      settings/page.tsx   # budget + notification prefs
    api/
      health/route.ts     # GET — health check, just return 200
      cron/
        fetch-usage/route.ts  # POST — protected by CRON_SECRET header
      providers/route.ts
      budgets/route.ts
  components/
    ui/                   # shadcn/ui primitives
    charts/               # recharts spend components
    emails/               # React Email templates
  lib/
    supabase/
      client.ts           # browser Supabase client
      server.ts           # server Supabase client
    providers/
      openai.ts           # fetch from OpenAI usage API
    encryption.ts         # AES-256-CBC encrypt/decrypt for API keys
    budget.ts             # budget status logic (green/amber/red)
  types/
    index.ts

## Coding Rules

### General
- Always TypeScript. No .js files except config files.
- Named exports everywhere except Next.js page/layout files.
- Never use `any`. Use proper types or `unknown`.
- Always handle loading + error states in every UI component.
- Keep components under 150 lines — split if larger.

### Next.js + Docker specific
- next.config.js MUST have output: 'standalone' — without this Docker build breaks.
- Never use Vercel-specific features: no vercel.json, no @vercel/og, no edge runtime.
- Use standard Node.js runtime for all API routes (not edge runtime).
- The /api/health route must return 200 OK — standard health check endpoint.
- Cron is handled by EC2 system cron job, not inline with the app.

### Supabase
- Never use Supabase client directly in components — use server actions or API routes.
- Server-side client uses SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY for cron routes only.
- Browser client uses NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY — this is safe to expose.
- Always check error after every Supabase query. Never assume success.
- Row Level Security is enabled — always query as the authenticated user.

### Security
- API keys must be AES-256-CBC encrypted before storing in DB.
- Cron endpoint must check: Authorization: Bearer CRON_SECRET header.
- All other API routes must verify user session first.
- Never return raw Supabase errors to client — log server-side, return generic message.
- SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is server-only — never in client code or NEXT_PUBLIC_.

### OpenAI Usage API
- Endpoint: GET https://api.openai.com/v1/usage?date=YYYY-MM-DD
- Auth header: Authorization: Bearer {decrypted_api_key}
- Map response to usage_records table shape.
- Always store the raw JSON response in the raw_response jsonb column for debugging.
- Handle rate limits with exponential backoff — wrap fetch in a retry helper.

### UI / Styling
- Tailwind utility classes only. No custom CSS.
- shadcn/ui for all base components.
- Budget status colors: green (#16a34a) = on track, amber (#d97706) = >80%, red (#dc2626) = over.
- Every page needs a loading.tsx and error.tsx.
- Desktop-first for Phase 2. Mobile responsiveness is Phase 3.

### Emails
- React Email templates in src/components/emails/.
- Send via Resend. From address must be a verified domain.
- Keep email templates simple — must render in Gmail on mobile.
- Daily alert email: show total spend, % of budget used, status badge, link to dashboard.

## Environment Variables
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
API_KEY_ENCRYPTION_SECRET=    # 32-char random string — generate: openssl rand -base64 32
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=
CRON_SECRET=                  # random string — generate: openssl rand -base64 32
SENTRY_DSN=

## Setting env vars on EC2
Place all variables in `/opt/ai-traker/.env.local` before running install.sh.
The install.sh script will automatically load these into the Docker container.

## Deploy Commands
SSH into your EC2 instance and run:
  cd /opt/ai-traker
  git pull origin main
  ./install.sh

This will rebuild the Docker image and restart the app.

## Cron Setup on EC2
The install.sh script automatically adds a midnight cron job.
To verify:
  crontab -l | grep fetch-usage

## What NOT to build in Phase 2
- No multi-provider support beyond OpenAI
- No team / org features
- No Stripe / payments
- No anomaly detection or forecasting
- No dark mode
- No mobile optimisation

## Phase 2 Done When
- User signs up, connects OpenAI key, sets monthly budget
- App fetches real OpenAI usage daily
- User gets daily email: spend vs budget, green/amber/red status
- Basic dashboard: total spend + daily cost chart
- 5 real users using it without founders helping them
